@startuml 02-device-pairing
title Protocol 02 - Pairing an additional device (SPAKE2, RFC 9382)

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName sans-serif
autonumber

actor "User" as user
participant "New device\n(e.g. ESP32)" as new
participant "Sync server" as srv
participant "Existing device\n(has account key AK)" as old

note over new, old
  Goal: give the new device a token and the account key AK
  without the server being able to learn AK.
  Two things connect the devices: the secret (128 random bits, made by
  the existing device, never sent to the server, the SPAKE2 password)
  and the channel (6 digits from the server, public, routes the request).
end note

user -> old : "Add a device"
old -> old : secret = 16 random bytes
old --> user : show QR code of\n<app URL>#join=<secret, base64url>
user -> new : scan the QR code (or open the link)
new -> new : take the secret from the URL fragment,\nremove it from address bar and history
new -> new : pake_a = SPAKE2 start (Ed25519, side A, password = secret,\nidentities "notes/pairing/new-device", "notes/pairing/existing-device")
new -> srv : POST /v1/pairings\n{ device_name, pake_msg: pake_a }
srv -> srv : create pairing, state = pending\nexpires in 5 minutes
srv --> new : 201 { pairing_id, channel, poll_token, expires_in }
new --> user : show the channel

user -> old : type the channel
old -> srv : POST /v1/pairings/claim\nAuthorization: Bearer token\n{ channel }
srv -> srv : state = claimed, bind to caller's account
srv --> old : 200 { pairing_id, device_name, pake_msg: pake_a }

old --> user : "Add device <device_name>?"
user -> old : confirm

old -> old : pake_b = SPAKE2 start (side B, password = secret)\nS = SPAKE2 finish(pake_a)\nK = HKDF-SHA256(S, info = "notes/pairing/v1/" | pairing_id)\nwrapped = AES-256-GCM(K, { account_id, account_key }, AAD = pairing_id)
old -> srv : POST /v1/pairings/:pairing_id/approve\nAuthorization: Bearer token\n{ pake_msg: pake_b, wrapped_account_key }
srv -> srv : store pake_b and wrapped key\nstate = approved
srv --> old : 204

loop every 2 s until approved or expired
  new -> srv : GET /v1/pairings/:pairing_id\nAuthorization: Bearer poll_token
  srv --> new : 200 { state: "pending" | "claimed" }
end

new -> srv : GET /v1/pairings/:pairing_id\nAuthorization: Bearer poll_token
srv -> srv : in one transaction: delete pairing row,\ncreate device, token = 256-bit random, store hash
srv --> new : 200 { state: "approved", account_id, device_id,\ntoken, pake_msg: pake_b, wrapped_account_key }

new -> new : S = SPAKE2 finish(pake_b), K as above\nopen wrapped_account_key with K

alt decryption succeeds and account_id matches
  new -> new : SecureStore: put AK, token, ids
  new --> user : "Paired"
  new -> srv : first sync from cursor 0
else decryption fails (wrong code or tampering)
  new -> srv : DELETE /v1/devices/:device_id\nAuthorization: Bearer token
  new -> new : discard token
  new --> user : "Pairing failed, try again"
end

note over srv
  What a malicious server can do: guess the 128-bit secret once per pairing.
  A wrong guess makes decryption fail on the new device.
  A device that started a pairing without the secret (the user was talked
  into typing its channel) gets a token but no key, and removes itself;
  an attacker's device would stay listed under Devices until removed.
  A pairing can be claimed once, and only the claiming account can approve it.
  The device and its token are created when the new device polls.
end note

@enduml
